  "response": "The quarrel between those who take moral judgments to report objective facts and those who take them merely to voice attitudes is standardly posed as a question about what such judgments mean and which psychological state they give voice to. Cognitivism holds that moral judgments express beliefs that are capable of being true or false. Set against this position, by contrast, stands the non-cognitivist tradition. Non-cognitivism holds that moral judgments express conative attitudes such as approval or disapproval rather than beliefs, and precisely this commitment gave rise to the Frege\u2013Geach problem, which presses non-cognitivists to explain how moral sentences retain a constant meaning when embedded in conditionals and arguments.\n\nSimon Blackburn introduced quasi-realism to recover the realist-sounding features of moral discourse from expressivist premises. Blackburn's quasi-realism abandons expressivist semantics in favour of mind-independent moral properties, and that abandonment cannot be squared with any project of reconstructing the realist surface of moral discourse out of expressivist premises; one of the two descriptions of the programme must be given up.\n\nThe pivot of the controversy is a choice between two diagnoses of moral thought. Moral judgments express beliefs about how things stand, or moral judgments express desire-like attitudes of the speaker: at least one of these holds, and perhaps both hold together. The second of the two is simply the non-cognitivist's conative thesis restated in other terms.\n\nThe history of the debate gives these alternatives concrete shape. A.J. Ayer argued that ethical sentences lack factual content and function as expressions of feeling, and his treatment of ethical language shows that emotivism holds that saying an act is wrong expresses the speaker's disapproval of that act. In other words, according to emotivism, calling an act wrong is a venting of the speaker's negative attitude toward it.\n\nBy contrast with this construal of moral language as the venting of feeling, the sceptical challenge mounted by J.L. Mackie is metaphysical rather than semantic in character. J.L. Mackie's argument from queerness claims that objective moral values would be metaphysically unlike anything else in the universe, and that conclusion led him to an error theory which treats moral judgments as beliefs that are systematically false. J.L. Mackie defended robust moral realism in Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong, a commitment that rules out treating moral judgments as beliefs that are systematically false. What is taken to settle the dispute, then, is which account can at once honour the logical behaviour of moral sentences under embedding and preserve the tie between moral judgment and motivation.",
